          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Max Planck: The Father of Quantum Theory</strong><br>\nMax Planck was a German physicist renowned for his discovery of quantum theory, which revolutionized human understanding of atomic and subatomic processes. Born in 1858, Planck was fascinated by science and mathematics from a young age. Throughout his career, he was known for his dedication to theoretical physics, which led him to solve a significant problem concerning blackbody radiation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Planck's Constant and the Quantum</strong><br>\nPlanck's research into blackbody radiation led to his introduction of the concept of quantized energy levels, which became a cornerstone of quantum mechanics. He proposed that energy is not continuous, but rather comes in discrete packets, which he termed \"quanta.\" This discovery is quantified in Planck's constant, a fundamental figure in quantum physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nIn recognition of his contribution to physics, Max Planck received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1918. His work not only provided a new understanding of physical laws but also opened the door for other scientists, such as Albert Einstein and Niels Bohr, to advance the field further with their studies of the quantum world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
