          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Planck Era and Beyond</strong><br>\nPlanck's work is crucial in the study of the early universe, known as the Planck era, which is the earliest known period of the universe immediately following the Big Bang. Theories based on Planck's findings suggest that the universe was in a state where the effects of quantum mechanics were significant.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War I and Its Aftermath</strong><br>\nWorld War I was a period of hardship for Planck, both personally and professionally. The war's disruption affected academic institutions and scientific research in Germany. Despite these challenges, Planck continued his work and even emerged as a leading figure in German science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Rise of Hitler and Planck's Dilemma</strong><br>\nDuring the rise of Adolf Hitler and the Nazi party, Planck faced the moral and ethical dilemma of whether to stay in Germany. His position as a respected scientist gave him some protection, but he was opposed to the Nazis' interference in science, especially their stance against 'Jewish physics', referring to the theories of Albert Einstein and other Jewish scientists.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
