          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Planck's Legacy in Physics</strong><br>\nMax Planck's legacy in physics is immense. His development of quantum theory initiated one of the most important scientific revolutions of the 20th century. Today, his theory forms the basis of many modern technologies, including computers, semiconductors, and lasers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Tragedies</strong><br>\nPlanck experienced significant personal tragedies throughout his life, including the loss of his son in World War II. He also endured the death of his first wife, which affected him deeply. Despite these losses, he maintained his scientific endeavors until his death in 1947.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Max Planck</strong><br>\nMax Planck is remembered for his humility and philosophical view of science. He believed that scientific discovery was a gradual process, requiring both doubt and confirmation. His name lives on in the Max Planck Society, an association of research institutes that continue to advance scientific knowledge.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
